<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zheng Xiaoxu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zheng Xiaoxu was a Chinese official, poet and calligrapher who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> and remained loyal to the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> after 1912. He helped bring Puyi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and became the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> in 1932. He resigned in 1935 after clashing with the Japanese authorities, who held real power, and died in 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-xiaoxu.jpg" alt="Zheng Xiaoxu" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zheng_Xiaoxu2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zheng was born on 2 May 1860 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, though his family traced its roots to Minhou, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. He obtained the intermediate degree in the imperial examinations in 1882 and three years later joined the secretariat of the statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>. In 1891 he became secretary to the Chinese legation in Tokyo and went on to consular duties in Tsukiji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a>, where he helped establish the Chinese guild. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> he met politicians and scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-munemitsu">Mutsu Munemitsu</a> and Naitō Torajirō.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> forced him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1894. He joined the secretariat of the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong">Zhang Zhidong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, followed him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and took a post in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, the Qing foreign office. After the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> in 1898 he held important posts in central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. When the imperial system collapsed in 1911 he refused to serve the Republic and retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, devoting himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, poetry and art and writing articles that called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> leaders thieves.</p><p>In 1923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to reorganise the imperial household. Zheng became a close adviser, helped arrange Puyi's flight to the foreign concession at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> after General Feng Yuxiang expelled him from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, and met Japanese officials and the Black Dragon Society to discuss a Qing restoration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> of 1931 he helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> and wrote the words of its national anthem. He hoped it would be a springboard for restoring the Qing across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a>, its real rulers, did not share that aim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pressed to resign after repeated clashes with the Japanese army leadership, he left office in May 1935. He died suddenly on 28 March 1938 in unclear circumstances, which led to speculation that the Japanese had poisoned him, and was given a state funeral in April. Remembered chiefly for his collaboration, he was also one of the most respected calligraphers of the 20th century: he supported himself in later life by selling his work, his style appears in the logos of Chinese companies, and historians still value his extensive diary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zheng Xiaoxu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To create an air of legitimacy the last emperor of China, Puyi, was brought in with his followers, among them the Qing loyalist <b>Zheng Xiaoxu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.3,120.62&amp;year=1910" aria-label="Suzhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zheng Xiaoxu"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5045 4889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5045 4889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.3,120.62&amp;year=1910">What was here in 1910? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
